1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,543
Every test passed.

2
00:00:01,543 --> 00:00:02,760
The release went out.

3
00:00:02,760 --> 00:00:06,004
And then five hundred people logged in at the same time.

4
00:00:06,554 --> 00:00:08,830
The page took twenty seconds to load.

5
00:00:08,830 --> 00:00:10,798
Nothing was wrong with the code.

6
00:00:10,798 --> 00:00:13,381
The code was just never tested under load.

7
00:00:13,931 --> 00:00:14,920
And last month,

8
00:00:14,920 --> 00:00:17,559
someone pasted a secret key into a file,

9
00:00:17,559 --> 00:00:20,000
and pushed it to a public repository.

10
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,781
No test ever looked for it.

11
00:00:22,331 --> 00:00:25,858
Today you learn the checks that sit beside your tests.

12
00:00:25,858 --> 00:00:26,445
How fast.

13
00:00:26,445 --> 00:00:27,555
How many at once.

14
00:00:27,555 --> 00:00:29,710
And is anything dangerous inside.

15
00:00:29,710 --> 00:00:31,343
You run a real load test,

16
00:00:31,343 --> 00:00:33,041
and a lab judges your own.

17
00:00:34,591 --> 00:00:36,020
Here is what to expect.

18
00:00:36,570 --> 00:00:40,965
One: the kinds of performance test. Performance, load, stress, and soak.

19
00:00:41,515 --> 00:00:46,742
Two: what to measure. Latency, throughput, and errors. And why we never trust an average.

20
00:00:47,292 --> 00:00:51,580
Three: security checks. Secrets, dependencies, code, and a running app.

21
00:00:52,130 --> 00:00:53,308
Then a live demo.

22
00:00:53,308 --> 00:00:54,418
A real load run,

23
00:00:54,418 --> 00:00:56,428
against a real small service.

24
00:00:56,428 --> 00:00:57,746
Then a secret scan,

25
00:00:57,746 --> 00:00:59,063
with a planted key.

26
00:00:59,613 --> 00:01:03,474
And a lab with six checks. You write the load script, and the scanner.

27
00:01:05,524 --> 00:01:07,404
Four kinds of performance test.

28
00:01:07,404 --> 00:01:11,284
They all ask one question: what happens when the system is busy?

29
00:01:11,834 --> 00:01:13,114
A performance test.

30
00:01:13,114 --> 00:01:13,721
One user,

31
00:01:13,721 --> 00:01:14,328
or a few.

32
00:01:14,328 --> 00:01:16,282
How fast is a single request?

33
00:01:16,282 --> 00:01:17,765
This is your baseline.

34
00:01:18,315 --> 00:01:19,083
A load test.

35
00:01:19,083 --> 00:01:21,899
The traffic you expect on a normal busy day.

36
00:01:21,899 --> 00:01:23,243
Fifty people at once,

37
00:01:23,243 --> 00:01:23,499
say.

38
00:01:23,499 --> 00:01:24,651
Does it stay fast?

39
00:01:25,201 --> 00:01:26,033
A stress test.

40
00:01:26,033 --> 00:01:27,282
More than you expect,

41
00:01:27,282 --> 00:01:28,651
until something breaks.

42
00:01:28,651 --> 00:01:29,840
You learn the limit,

43
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:31,327
and how it fails: slowly,

44
00:01:31,327 --> 00:01:32,219
or all at once.

45
00:01:32,769 --> 00:01:38,721
A soak test. Normal load, for hours. It finds the slow leaks: memory, connections, disk.

46
00:01:39,271 --> 00:01:43,986
Start small. A load test on every release beats a giant test once a year.

47
00:01:45,536 --> 00:01:47,307
What do you measure? Three numbers.

48
00:01:47,857 --> 00:01:48,362
Latency.

49
00:01:48,362 --> 00:01:50,069
How long one request takes,

50
00:01:50,069 --> 00:01:51,081
in milliseconds.

51
00:01:51,081 --> 00:01:52,788
Do not look at the average.

52
00:01:52,788 --> 00:01:54,747
An average hides the slow ones.

53
00:01:55,297 --> 00:01:56,854
Look at the p95.

54
00:01:56,854 --> 00:01:57,992
Sort all the times.

55
00:01:57,992 --> 00:02:01,585
Ninety-five out of a hundred requests were faster than this.

56
00:02:01,585 --> 00:02:03,681
It is what your unlucky users feel.

57
00:02:04,231 --> 00:02:08,221
Throughput. Requests per second. How much work the system really finishes.

58
00:02:08,771 --> 00:02:14,253
Error rate. How many requests failed. A fast answer that is an error is not a fast answer.

59
00:02:14,803 --> 00:02:16,019
Then set a budget.

60
00:02:16,019 --> 00:02:19,193
p95 under three hundred milliseconds,

61
00:02:19,193 --> 00:02:20,273
and zero errors.

62
00:02:20,273 --> 00:02:23,379
A budget turns a number into a pass or a fail.

63
00:02:24,929 --> 00:02:25,712
Now security.

64
00:02:25,712 --> 00:02:27,578
You do not need to be a hacker.

65
00:02:27,578 --> 00:02:29,505
You need a few automatic checks,

66
00:02:29,505 --> 00:02:30,348
on every push.

67
00:02:30,898 --> 00:02:31,840
A secrets scan.

68
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:34,604
Look for keys and passwords inside the code.

69
00:02:34,604 --> 00:02:36,300
This is the cheapest check,

70
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:37,682
and the most valuable.

71
00:02:38,232 --> 00:02:39,396
A dependency scan.

72
00:02:39,396 --> 00:02:41,983
Your code uses other people's libraries.

73
00:02:41,983 --> 00:02:44,312
Is a version known to be vulnerable?

74
00:02:44,862 --> 00:02:50,216
A code scan. A tool reads your code, and warns about risky patterns. Without running it.

75
00:02:50,766 --> 00:02:52,292
A scan of the running app.

76
00:02:52,292 --> 00:02:53,817
A tool probes a test copy,

77
00:02:53,817 --> 00:02:55,166
like an attacker would,

78
00:02:55,166 --> 00:02:55,929
from outside.

79
00:02:56,479 --> 00:02:58,188
The idea is to shift left.

80
00:02:58,188 --> 00:03:01,016
Find the problem on the developer's laptop,

81
00:03:01,016 --> 00:03:02,265
or in the pipeline.

82
00:03:02,265 --> 00:03:03,448
Not in production,

83
00:03:03,448 --> 00:03:04,500
from a stranger.

84
00:03:06,050 --> 00:03:11,149
Go to the lab folder.

85
00:03:12,699 --> 00:03:14,597
Look around.

86
00:03:15,147 --> 00:03:17,850
A small service is already running here.

87
00:03:17,850 --> 00:03:19,066
And a demo folder,

88
00:03:19,066 --> 00:03:20,687
with a tiny load script.

89
00:03:20,687 --> 00:03:21,092
First,

90
00:03:21,092 --> 00:03:21,903
one request,

91
00:03:21,903 --> 00:03:22,443
by hand.

92
00:03:22,993 --> 00:03:27,260
One request.

93
00:03:28,610 --> 00:03:32,685
The answer, and twenty milliseconds. That is one user. Our baseline.

94
00:03:33,235 --> 00:03:39,144
Read the script.

95
00:03:39,694 --> 00:03:40,099
Short.

96
00:03:40,099 --> 00:03:40,909
Ten workers,

97
00:03:40,909 --> 00:03:41,922
sixty requests.

98
00:03:41,922 --> 00:03:43,205
It times every one,

99
00:03:43,205 --> 00:03:43,947
sorts them,

100
00:03:43,947 --> 00:03:46,513
and takes the ninety-fifth percentile.

101
00:03:46,513 --> 00:03:47,660
It counts errors.

102
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:50,766
And it exits with one if the budget is broken.

103
00:03:51,316 --> 00:03:54,111
Run the load.

104
00:03:55,461 --> 00:03:56,579
Sixty requests,

105
00:03:56,579 --> 00:03:57,473
ten at once.

106
00:03:57,473 --> 00:03:58,516
p95,

107
00:03:58,516 --> 00:04:00,380
about fifty milliseconds.

108
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:01,274
Zero errors.

109
00:04:01,274 --> 00:04:01,796
Notice,

110
00:04:01,796 --> 00:04:06,118
ten users at once is slower than one: twenty became fifty.

111
00:04:06,118 --> 00:04:07,087
That is load.

112
00:04:07,637 --> 00:04:12,992
A slower path.

113
00:04:13,542 --> 00:04:17,361
p95, five hundred and sixteen. Far over the budget.

114
00:04:17,911 --> 00:04:19,895
The exit code.

115
00:04:20,445 --> 00:04:26,226
One. The budget gate failed, exactly like a failing test. In CI, this is a red cross.

116
00:04:26,776 --> 00:04:33,688
Scan for secrets.

117
00:04:34,238 --> 00:04:36,585
Nothing printed. Check the exit code.

118
00:04:37,135 --> 00:04:39,119
Type: echo, dollar, question mark.

119
00:04:39,669 --> 00:04:43,253
One. For grep, one means: found nothing. That is good news here.

120
00:04:43,803 --> 00:04:47,451
Plant a key.

121
00:04:49,401 --> 00:04:51,790
Scan again.

122
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:53,906
Found.

123
00:04:53,906 --> 00:04:54,455
The file,

124
00:04:54,455 --> 00:04:55,248
and the line.

125
00:04:55,248 --> 00:04:56,896
A pipeline would fail here,

126
00:04:56,896 --> 00:04:59,641
and the key would never reach the repository.

127
00:05:00,191 --> 00:05:02,815
Clean up.

128
00:05:03,365 --> 00:05:08,720
Where am I?

129
00:05:09,270 --> 00:05:13,089
Zero out of six. That is your starting line. Honest, and useful.

130
00:05:13,639 --> 00:05:15,601
Clean up.

131
00:05:16,151 --> 00:05:19,693
Removed. The service is stopped too. Nothing left behind.

132
00:05:21,243 --> 00:05:25,723
Your lab task: write a real load check, and a secrets scanner. Six checks.

133
00:05:26,273 --> 00:05:28,056
One: write load.py.

134
00:05:28,056 --> 00:05:30,581
It uses only the standard library,

135
00:05:30,581 --> 00:05:32,215
threads and urllib.

136
00:05:32,215 --> 00:05:34,295
It runs and writes a report,

137
00:05:34,295 --> 00:05:34,592
out,

138
00:05:34,592 --> 00:05:35,038
/

139
00:05:35,038 --> 00:05:36,449
report.json.

140
00:05:36,999 --> 00:05:39,803
Two and three: the report has the right keys,

141
00:05:39,803 --> 00:05:40,738
sixty requests,

142
00:05:40,738 --> 00:05:41,486
ten workers.

143
00:05:41,486 --> 00:05:46,471
And the p95 must be the real ninety-fifth percentile of your own list.

144
00:05:47,021 --> 00:05:51,480
Four: a four hundred and four counts as an error, and the run exits with one.

145
00:05:52,030 --> 00:05:55,891
Five: a budget flag. Cart passes. The slow path fails.

146
00:05:56,441 --> 00:05:57,827
Six: scan.sh.

147
00:05:57,827 --> 00:05:59,522
A clean folder passes.

148
00:05:59,522 --> 00:06:00,600
A planted key,

149
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:02,294
or a planted password,

150
00:06:02,294 --> 00:06:02,756
fails.

151
00:06:02,756 --> 00:06:03,912
Then lab check,

152
00:06:03,912 --> 00:06:05,529
until six out of six.

153
00:06:07,579 --> 00:06:10,374
Where to find the lab. Pause here, and write it down.

154
00:06:10,924 --> 00:06:12,388
One: the repository,

155
00:06:12,388 --> 00:06:16,708
github.com/it-waves/devops-ai,

156
00:06:16,708 --> 00:06:19,052
in the folder academy/lab.

157
00:06:19,602 --> 00:06:25,554
Two: the files, lab142.sh, and shortcuts142.sh.

158
00:06:26,104 --> 00:06:31,565
Three: install it, with sudo install, into /usr/local/sbin/.

159
00:06:32,115 --> 00:06:33,973
Four: load the short names,

160
00:06:33,973 --> 00:06:37,002
with source shortcuts142.sh.

161
00:06:37,002 --> 00:06:37,828
Then lab up,

162
00:06:37,828 --> 00:06:38,516
lab check,

163
00:06:38,516 --> 00:06:39,411
and lab down.

164
00:06:39,961 --> 00:06:43,502
Five: you only need Python 3. Nothing else to install.

165
00:06:44,052 --> 00:06:47,788
Six: it only touches /opt/lab142,

166
00:06:47,788 --> 00:06:51,388
and a small service on local port eight one four two.

167
00:06:51,388 --> 00:06:53,290
Lab down removes everything.

168
00:06:53,840 --> 00:06:55,482
The link is in the description.

169
00:06:57,532 --> 00:07:01,180
Pass or fail rubric. Six points. All six to pass.

170
00:07:01,730 --> 00:07:04,653
One: load.py runs, and writes the report.

171
00:07:05,203 --> 00:07:10,024
Two: the report has all the keys, sixty requests, ten workers, zero errors.

172
00:07:10,574 --> 00:07:13,347
Three: p95 is the real percentile.

173
00:07:13,897 --> 00:07:17,311
Four: a four hundred and four is an error, and fails the run.

174
00:07:17,861 --> 00:07:21,914
Five: the budget gate passes on cart, and fails on the slow path.

175
00:07:22,464 --> 00:07:27,776
Six: the scanner passes a clean folder, and fails a planted key and a planted password.

176
00:07:28,326 --> 00:07:29,627
Pass means all six.

177
00:07:31,677 --> 00:07:33,448
Your turn, on your own machine.

178
00:07:33,998 --> 00:07:37,560
One: lab up. Read the task file. Try the service with curl.

179
00:07:38,110 --> 00:07:43,038
Two: write the load script, step by step. First one request, then many, with threads.

180
00:07:43,588 --> 00:07:48,196
Three: time each one, sort the times, and take the percentile. Write the report.

181
00:07:48,746 --> 00:07:53,034
Four: add the error count, and the budget exit code. Then write the scanner.

182
00:07:53,584 --> 00:07:57,062
Five: lab check, until six out of six. Then lab down.

183
00:07:57,612 --> 00:07:59,446
Pause the video, and begin now.

184
00:08:14,996 --> 00:08:15,871
Quick check.

185
00:08:16,421 --> 00:08:19,429
What is the difference between a load test and a stress test?

186
00:08:19,979 --> 00:08:22,603
Why look at the p95, not the average?

187
00:08:23,153 --> 00:08:25,670
What is a budget, and what does it do in CI?

188
00:08:26,220 --> 00:08:29,079
Name two security checks that need no running app.

189
00:08:29,629 --> 00:08:31,835
Objective covered: MC15,

190
00:08:31,835 --> 00:08:32,974
objective seven.

191
00:08:32,974 --> 00:08:33,828
Performance,

192
00:08:33,828 --> 00:08:34,184
load,

193
00:08:34,184 --> 00:08:35,679
and security testing,

194
00:08:35,679 --> 00:08:37,458
and what each one proves.

195
00:08:38,008 --> 00:08:40,077
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

196
00:08:50,627 --> 00:08:54,425
Next: flaky tests, and tests that depend on their environment.

197
00:08:54,975 --> 00:08:58,303
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
